; A `Map` carries no element type, and the default argument must not be
; allowed to name one for a value it did not supply.
;
; `mapGet` answered `(-> Int Int a a)` until 2026-08-25. The variable is
; in a PARAMETER, so `AX3040` does not fire — the rule asks whether a
; parameter mentions it, and one does. What the rule cannot see is that
; the parameter mentioning it is a DEFAULT, used only when the key is
; absent, while the value actually returned comes out of a table that
; carries no type at all. `dflt` witnesses the absent path and nothing
; else, and the cast was on the other one.
;
; Measured on the compiler before the change: `check` answered OK and
; the binary exited 139 — 100000000 dereferenced as a String pointer.
;
; `mapGet` answers `Int` now, so the mismatch is an ordinary `AX3004` at
; the argument, which is the diagnostic a reader can act on: the default
; is an `Int` and `"absent"` is not. `mapGetStr` is the reader for a
; table whose words really are Strings, and it says so at the call.
;
; `honest` is the control: the same lookup with an `Int` default, which
; is what every other call site in this repository already writes, and
; it must draw nothing.
;
; The handle parameter is spelled `Map`, not `Int`, since 2026-09-03:
; `mapGet`'s first argument is a nominal type now, so `Int` here would
; add three AX3004s of its own and bury the one this case is about.
; tests/diagnostics/483-map-handle-not-int.ax is where that rule is
; tested on purpose.
(import Map)

(import Str)

(:: mixed (-> Map Int))

(fn (mixed n)
  {
    (mapInsert n 1 100000000)
    (strLen
      (mapGet n 1 "absent")
    )
  }
)

(:: honest (-> Map Int))

(fn (honest n)
  {
    (mapInsert n 1 100000000)
    (mapGet n 1 (- 0 1))
  }
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) 0)
